                    <physDesc>
                        <objectDesc>
                            <supportDesc>
                                <support><p>A large quadrangular base of grey limestone. The block is essentially intact, but with partial loss of the front upper right corner (including part of the upper right of the front face) and substantial loss of the rear upper left corner. The upper surface is additionally partially eroded in the rear right quarter. The right side has a vertical line of red paint from top to bottom some 2/3 of the way towards the rear, and the side of the block is covered with white paint from the red line to the rear edge (whether either red or white paint is ancient or modern is unknown). Although the upper surface is only partially preserved, the remains of two holes are clearly visible (presumably the footholes/fixing holes for a standing statue): a deep circular depression towards the left rear of the upper surface, 8cm in diameter and c.7.5 cm deep originally (59 cm from the front, 23 cm from the left edge, 16 cm from the rear); and a deep elliptical hole toward the front right of the upper surface, c.6.5 cm deep, 4.5-5 cm wide, and at least 15.5 cm long (the front part of the hole is lost; 48 cm from rear edge, 37 cm from right side).</p>
                                    <material ana="#material.inorganic.stone.sedimentary_rock.limestone.calcite_limestone.grey.calcare-lucidabile_grigio_sicilia-orientale" type="stone.limestone.polishable.grey" subtype="calcare_grigio_SiciliaOR_taormina" resp="#Coccato" ref="http://www.eagle-network.eu/voc/material/lod/66.html">massive light grey limestone, likely calcare di Taormina (DOA) (ooidal/peloidal grainstone with sparite veins) (identification based on pXRF, digital microscopy, XRD)
