    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition" subtype="primary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="grc" resp="#JP" source="#autopsy">
                <ab>
                    <lb n="1"/><roleName type="supracivic" subtype="basileus"><w n="5">Βασιλέος</w></roleName> <roleName type="supracivic" subtype="hegemon"><w n="10">ἁγε<supplied reason="lost">ομένου</supplied></w></roleName>
                    <lb n="2"/><persName type="attested"><name><w n="20">̒Ιέρωνος</w></name> <persName type="attested"><name type="patronym"><w n="25">Ἱεροκλέος</w></name></persName></persName>
                    <lb n="3"/><placeName type="ethnic"><w n="30">Συρακόσιοι</w></placeName> <w n="35">θεοῖς</w> <w n="40">πᾶσι</w>
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus">
                <listApp>
                    <app>
                        <note>Text from autopsy</note>
                    </app>
                    <app loc="Line 1">
                        <note>Lupus: ἁγε[μονεύοντος]; Kaibel: ἁγε[μόνος]; Oikonomedes: ἁγέ[τορος]</note>
                    </app>
                </listApp>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en" resp="#JP">
                <p>When Hieron son of Hierokles was leading, as king, the Syracusans (dedicated this) to all the gods.</p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary" resp="#JP">
                <p>The reading of line 1 is much debated, whether it should be the substantive ἁγε[μόνος], or a participle, either ἁγε[μονεύοντος], or ἁγε[ομένου]. As John Ma, Statues and Cities (Oxford 2013) 20 n.27 notes, if the substantive is retained, this is a completely anomalous instance of an honorific dedication where the honorand is in the genitive (and, has been noted by Dittenberger the absence of a conjunction would also be odd); consequently, it seems much more likely, as Dittenberger already argued, that a genitive absolute should be understood in line 1, similar to standard formulations recording the presiding magistrate or similar. As Dittenberger also, rightly, noted, there is insufficient space on the stone for ἁγε[μονεύοντος] and so ἁγε[ομένου] seems much the most likely reading (the surface is sufficiently damaged across the end of line 1 as to make it impossible to judge whethere there was any sort of vacat at the end of the line; ἁγε[ομένου] would fill almost the whole available space (c.25.5 cm, against the preceding 55cm of text and 4.5 cm of initial vacat). The holes on the upper surface do however appear to be entirely compatible with a portrait statue (pace Dimartino), and for all that the formulation is untypical, the purpose must surely be an honorific for Hieron, given that he is the entire focus of the text.</p>
                <p>The suggestion of Oikonomides based on an Epirot text, in Oikonomides, A.N. 1987. The Inscriptions of two dedications by Epirot royalty. In P. Cabanes (ed.), L’Illyrie méridionale et l’Épire dans l’Antiquité (Clermont-Ferrand), 121-4, at 123-4 to read ἁγέ[τορος] is critiqued by Marchetti, P. 1992. Témoignages épigraphiques concernant Pyrrhus. In T. Hackens et al. (eds), The Age of Pyrrhus (Archeologia Transatlantica XI) (Providence RI), 51-72, at 59-61.</p>
            </div>
